Which is why a script rather than a macro is what's needed. 
Specifically it needs to be able to test the result of a command 
and then conditionally continue. ISTM this may be possible for TTY 
builds on Mac OS X with AppleScript, but not for the Qt build as 
the contents of it's message pane aren't selectable.

What would worry me most about invoking a script/macro is that 
something else that would make a player stop might not be checked 
by the macro [like that arch-lich that teleported in while the 
macro was searching a wall]. Short of parsing the entire map with ; 
commands between every action (or the currently-detectable/viewable 
area if the player doesn't have telepathy), I don't see this 
concern being addressable.

i.e. going into 'auto-pilot' kills players, invoking a macro might 
lead to the same problem.

> Then again, a simple define that could be put under the F10 key would be
> useful. Even rogue had that built in.

I assume this was some means of assigning a string of commands to a 
